g the the Covey  pandemic and I've worked shift since as  well so um and dealing with the backlog  caused by covid is you know ex  exceptional hard work what are their  concerns then so pay obviously is is one  of those but I think it's much more than  pay I think it's about the long hours  that nurses work often not finishing on  time they don't get protected Learning  and Development time like doctors do for  example and skill mix for example on the  wards it can be it's not just about  numbers on the walls it's the level  experience and skills that are available  too so when I've met with Pat Cullen for  example they're the sorts of issues that  the Secretary of State wants to talk  about in terms of improving the working  conditions of nurses and pay is just a  tiny element and I think the strike  action is more about um from talking to  my colleagues is more about their  frustration about working conditions  rather than pay itself so even though  you don't you say you don't personally  agree with strong connection you've got  Sympathy For What someone else I think  nurses have got you know have had a a  very difficult time I don't think  anyone's denying that I just don't see  what's striking achieves I'm very much  you know Keen to work with Pat and the  RCN to look at those conditions as is  the secretary State when he met with her  this week but we cannot meet the demands  of a 19 pay rate just talking um you  know about  um Pat Cullen and about the meeting with  Steve Barkley because she was pretty  disparaging about what came out of that  meeting she said that Steve Barkley  showed belligerence he refused to  discuss pay he was nothing short of  disrespectful I also thought it was  interesting to say that she thought the  government was displaying Macho Behavior  towards the RCM because it sees nursing  as female work that it doesn't value  properly I mean it doesn't sound like  you know she really believes the house  secretary has got her back well I'm  s